  "manufacturing_and_cost_judgment": {
    "2027": "Server units grow30%, consistent with Intel management industry-growth commentary but not guaranteed Intel acceptance. Added substrates/packaging working capital and physical capital fund the stronger output. Client weakness remains. Foundry still loses3.5B.",
    "2028": "Units grow25%; delayed installed equipment and packaging investments reach output, allowing Foundry breakeven one year later than some unverified public bull narratives. This is owned judgment, not confirmed management guidance.",
    "2029_2030": "Server volume growth decelerates18/12%; Foundry earns2/3.5B despite D&A19/21B and continuing node R&D. Physical capital30/29B remains above base.",
    "product_margin_boundary": "The modest0.5\u20131percentage-point DCAI margin improvement is CPU mix and product selling/R&D leverage at existing market-like transfer charges. Wafer yield, cycle time and factory absorption savings appear only in Foundry. No repeat inventory reserve release is credited.",
    "post2030": "DCAI product margin converges to base32% by2040, Foundry to3% Products by2035, and incremental capital productivity to the same0.60 maturity convention. Continuous nodes and packaging are still funded. Higher terminal dollar profit comes from the earlier accepted volume level, not permanently higher margins or a cheaper uncalibrated terminal capital ratio.",
    "corporate_constraint": "Base corporate and ordinary SBC policy retained as an explicit marginal-organization assumption. Incremental manufacturing and product R&D costs are inside segment profits. If the faster ramp requires additional central compensation beyond that policy, this countercase overstates profit until charged."
  },
